__d(function(e,t,a,o,n,i,r){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"English",textbook:"A Pact with the Sun",chapter_number:8,chapter_title:"A Pact with the Sun",quiz_type:"MCQ only",mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is the condition of Saeeda's mother at the beginning of the story?",options:["A) She is healthy but very old and weak","B) She is seriously ill and bedridden in a dark, stuffy room","C) She is recovering well after surgery","D) She is ill but able to walk around the house"],correct_answer:"B) She is seriously ill and bedridden in a dark, stuffy room",explanation:"At the beginning of the story, Saeeda's mother has been ill for a long time and is confined to a dark, airless room on the old doctor's orders. Despite expensive medicines, she is not recovering. This is the central problem the story seeks to resolve."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Why does the old doctor forbid opening the window in Saeeda's mother's room?",options:["A) He believes cold air will worsen her respiratory illness","B) He wants to keep the patient dependent on his expensive medicines for financial gain","C) The room faces a noisy street that would disturb the patient","D) He follows old-fashioned medical advice that was popular in his time"],correct_answer:"B) He wants to keep the patient dependent on his expensive medicines for financial gain",explanation:"The story makes the old doctor's motivation clear \u2014 he is greedy. By keeping the room dark and airless, he prevents the very thing (sunlight and fresh air) that would cure the patient, ensuring she remains ill and continues to pay for his visits and medicines. This is an ethical betrayal of his duty as a healer."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What does the new, kind doctor recommend as the primary treatment for Saeeda's mother?",options:["A) A stronger dose of the existing medicines","B) Complete bed rest in a quiet, dark room","C) Fresh air and sunlight through an open window","D) A change of location to a hill station"],correct_answer:"C) Fresh air and sunlight through an open window",explanation:"The new doctor immediately recognises that the dark, stuffy room is the problem. He prescribes the simple but correct treatment: open the window to allow sunlight and fresh air. This reflects the medically sound principle of heliotherapy and ventilation as aids to recovery."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What does Saeeda do after the new doctor's visit to help her mother?",options:["A) She goes to the market to buy herbal medicines","B) She speaks to the last rays of the setting sun and pleads with them to visit her mother's room","C) She writes a letter to the old doctor asking him to change his advice","D) She prays at a nearby temple for her mother's recovery"],correct_answer:"B) She speaks to the last rays of the setting sun and pleads with them to visit her mother's room",explanation:"Saeeda goes to the garden in the evening and speaks directly to the sun rays, pleading with them to shine into her mother's room the next morning. This extraordinary act, driven by pure love and determination, is the emotional climax of the story and leads to the pact."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is the meaning of 'pact' as used in the chapter title?",options:["A) A scientific agreement between a doctor and a patient","B) A formal promise or agreement between two parties","C) A prayer offered to a god","D) A legal contract signed by two people"],correct_answer:"B) A formal promise or agreement between two parties",explanation:"A pact is a promise or agreement. In the story, Saeeda makes a pact with the sun rays \u2014 she asks them to shine into her mother's room, and they promise to do so. Despite threatening clouds, the sun rays honour their pact the next morning."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which literary device is used when the sun rays listen to Saeeda and make a promise?",options:["A) Simile","B) Metaphor","C) Personification","D) Alliteration"],correct_answer:"C) Personification",explanation:"Personification is the literary device of giving human qualities (speech, thought, emotion) to non-human things. The sun rays are personified \u2014 they listen to Saeeda, feel moved by her love, and make a promise. This makes the natural world respond to human love and determination."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What obstacle do the sun rays face before keeping their promise to Saeeda?",options:["A) A heavy rainstorm during the night","B) Dark clouds that threaten to block the sun the next morning","C) The old doctor who locks the window again","D) Saeeda's mother asks for the window to stay closed"],correct_answer:"B) Dark clouds that threaten to block the sun the next morning",explanation:"Dark clouds gather overnight, threatening to prevent the sun from shining into the room. However, the rays wake early and push through the clouds, keeping their promise to Saeeda. The clouds represent the obstacles that love and determination must overcome."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What happens when sunlight finally enters Saeeda's mother's room?",options:["A) Her mother immediately wakes up and is fully cured","B) Her mother sits in the warm sunlight and fresh air and begins to feel genuinely better","C) The old doctor comes and closes the window again","D) Saeeda's mother asks for the curtains to be drawn because the light is too bright"],correct_answer:"B) Her mother sits in the warm sunlight and fresh air and begins to feel genuinely better",explanation:"When morning sunlight floods the room, Saeeda's mother sits in its warmth and breathes the fresh air. She begins to feel better for the first time \u2014 showing that sunlight and fresh air were indeed the real cure, validating both the new doctor's advice and Saeeda's determination."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What does Vitamin D, produced by the body when exposed to sunlight, help with?",options:["A) Digestion of food and absorption of water","B) Strong bones, a healthy immune system, and mental wellbeing","C) Production of red blood cells and haemoglobin","D) Regulation of body temperature during fever"],correct_answer:"B) Strong bones, a healthy immune system, and mental wellbeing",explanation:"Vitamin D is produced when skin is exposed to ultraviolet B rays in sunlight. It is essential for calcium absorption (strong bones), immune system function, and mental health. Saeeda's mother, confined in a dark room, would have had critically low Vitamin D, worsening her illness."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which Karnataka tradition of morning sun exposure connects to the health lesson of this chapter?",options:["A) Ugadi festival celebrations at sunrise","B) Surya Namaskara (Sun Salutation) practised at dawn","C) The Mysore Dasara procession in morning light","D) Lighting lamps at the Udupi Krishna Temple at sunrise"],correct_answer:"B) Surya Namaskara (Sun Salutation) practised at dawn",explanation:"Surya Namaskara is an ancient Indian yoga practice performed at sunrise, facing the sun. Widely practised across Karnataka, it maximises early morning sun exposure and reflects the traditional Indian recognition that sunlight is essential for health \u2014 directly connecting to this chapter's scientific and moral lesson."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"The old doctor's behaviour in the story is best described as which of the following?",options:["A) Ignorant \u2014 he genuinely does not know that sunlight would help","B) Cautious \u2014 he is being careful not to expose a sick patient to cold air","C) Unethical \u2014 he deliberately withholds the correct treatment for financial gain","D) Experienced \u2014 he uses traditional methods that have worked before"],correct_answer:"C) Unethical \u2014 he deliberately withholds the correct treatment for financial gain",explanation:"The story makes clear that the old doctor's motive is financial. He is not ignorant \u2014 the new doctor's immediate diagnosis shows that the correct treatment was obvious. The old doctor chooses profit over the patient's wellbeing, making his behaviour an ethical failure, not a medical one."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What is heliotherapy, and how does it relate to the story?",options:["A) Treatment using herbal medicines extracted from sun-grown plants","B) Medical treatment using sunlight, historically used for respiratory diseases","C) A type of surgery performed in brightly lit operating rooms","D) Therapy involving heat lamps used indoors as a substitute for sunlight"],correct_answer:"B) Medical treatment using sunlight, historically used for respiratory diseases",explanation:"Heliotherapy is medical treatment using sunlight. Before antibiotics, placing respiratory patients in sunny, well-ventilated rooms was standard treatment. The new doctor's prescription \u2014 open the window for sunlight \u2014 is based on this principle, giving the story a real scientific foundation beyond its emotional and allegorical content."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What does Saeeda's act of speaking to the sun rays symbolise in the story?",options:["A) That children can communicate with nature through special powers","B) That love and determination can move even the greatest forces of nature","C) That sunlight is a god that responds to prayer","D) That Saeeda is more intelligent than the doctors who treated her mother"],correct_answer:"B) That love and determination can move even the greatest forces of nature",explanation:"Saeeda's pact with the sun rays is a symbolic act. It represents that pure, selfless love and determined action can achieve what adult logic, expensive medicine, and passive acceptance cannot. The sun responding is a literary metaphor for how love and determination change outcomes that seemed fixed."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which institution in Bengaluru treats respiratory patients and endorses sunlight and open-air therapy as part of recovery?",options:["A) NIMHANS","B) IISc","C) RGICD (Rajiv Gandhi Institute of Chest Diseases)","D) S-VYASA (Swami Vivekananda Yoga University)"],correct_answer:"C) RGICD (Rajiv Gandhi Institute of Chest Diseases)",explanation:"The Rajiv Gandhi Institute of Chest Diseases (RGICD) in Bengaluru is one of India's leading hospitals for respiratory conditions. It endorses sunlight exposure and open-air therapy as part of recovery \u2014 directly validating the new doctor's prescription in the story and the chapter's health message."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"The contrast between the old and new doctors is used to develop which central theme?",options:["A) Modern medicine is better than traditional medicine","B) Older doctors are always less skilled than younger ones","C) Greed versus compassion in professional and personal life","D) The importance of getting a second medical opinion"],correct_answer:"C) Greed versus compassion in professional and personal life",explanation:"The two doctors are not contrasted by age or skill \u2014 they are contrasted by motivation. The old doctor is driven by greed; the new doctor by genuine care. This is the story's central moral: greed withholds the cure, while compassion delivers it. The contrast applies beyond medicine to any situation involving selfishness versus real care."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following best explains why the story personifies the sun rays rather than using a human helper?",options:["A) Because the author did not want to add more human characters to an already large cast","B) To create a magical, emotionally resonant experience and honour the Indian tradition of nature as a living, healing presence","C) Because no human character in the story was able to help Saeeda","D) To teach students about the science of sunlight through a fictional character"],correct_answer:"B) To create a magical, emotionally resonant experience and honour the Indian tradition of nature as a living, healing presence",explanation:"Personifying the sun makes the story magical and emotionally powerful for young readers. It also connects to India's deep cultural tradition of treating nature as a living, responsive presence \u2014 seen in practices like Surya Namaskara and festivals honouring rivers, trees, and the sun. It makes the healing feel earned by love, not just medically explained."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What does Saeeda's mother's illness in a dark room represent at the symbolic level of the story?",options:["A) The dangers of living in an urban environment without green spaces","B) How greed and wrong treatment can keep a person trapped in suffering they could be freed from","C) The weakness of women who depend on others for medical care","D) The failure of the Indian healthcare system to provide good doctors"],correct_answer:"B) How greed and wrong treatment can keep a person trapped in suffering they could be freed from",explanation:"Symbolically, the mother's confinement in a dark, airless room represents how greed (the old doctor's) can trap someone in unnecessary suffering. The cure was always available \u2014 free, natural, and simple. But selfishness prevented it. The room's darkness is a metaphor for the darkness greed causes in people's lives."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Why is it medically accurate that fresh air and sunlight help in recovery from illness?",options:["A) Fresh air cools the body and prevents fevers from rising","B) Sunlight kills harmful bacteria on the skin, and fresh air improves oxygen supply to the lungs","C) Sunlight warms the muscles and relieves the pain of illness","D) Fresh air increases appetite, helping the patient eat more and gain strength"],correct_answer:"B) Sunlight kills harmful bacteria on the skin, and fresh air improves oxygen supply to the lungs",explanation:"This is medically accurate \u2014 ultraviolet rays in sunlight have germicidal (bacteria-killing) properties on the skin, and sunlight triggers Vitamin D production. Fresh outdoor air contains higher oxygen levels and fewer indoor pollutants, directly supporting respiratory recovery. Both work together to accelerate healing."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"The story ends with the hope of the mother's recovery, not a complete cure. Why might the author choose to end on hope rather than a guaranteed cure?",options:["A) Because the author did not know whether sunlight could fully cure the illness","B) Because hope \u2014 not certainty \u2014 is what love and determination actually produce, making the ending more honest and meaningful","C) Because the story needs a sequel to show the full recovery","D) Because complete recovery would make the old doctor's mistake seem too severe"],correct_answer:"B) Because hope \u2014 not certainty \u2014 is what love and determination actually produce, making the ending more honest and meaningful",explanation:"Ending on hope rather than certain cure makes the story emotionally truthful. Love and determination cannot guarantee outcomes \u2014 but they change the direction of events toward the better. The mother beginning to feel better is already a transformation from hopeless confinement to possible recovery. Hope is the authentic reward of real love."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"The old doctor and the crocodile in Chapter 6 both betray someone who trusted them. What common lesson do these two characters teach?",options:["A) That all doctors and crocodiles are untrustworthy","B) That greed corrupts relationships and betrays the trust placed in us, and that the betrayer ultimately gains nothing","C) That physical strength or professional authority always leads to corruption","D) That children are always more honest than adults"],correct_answer:"B) That greed corrupts relationships and betrays the trust placed in us, and that the betrayer ultimately gains nothing",explanation:"Both the old doctor and the crocodile are motivated by greed and betray those who trusted them. Both ultimately gain nothing \u2014 the crocodile loses his friend and the wish, and the old doctor's patient gets cured by a different doctor. This cross-chapter connection shows that betrayal driven by greed always backfires on the betrayer."}]}},2763,[]);